          {
            "kind": "heading",
            "id": "ds310",
            "text": "Amazon S3 bucket basics",
            "level": 1
          },
          {
            "kind": "styled",
            "id": "ds311",
            "text": "Create a bucket, upload an object, and read it back with the CLI.",
            "style": {
              "size": 16,
              "color": "#545b64"
            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "ds312",
            "text": "1. Bucket names are globally unique and DNS-compatible: lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens, 3 to 63 characters. Choose the Region closest to the application that will read the data."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "ds313",
            "text": "2. Block Public Access is enabled by default. Leave it on and grant access with IAM policies or presigned URLs; only turn it off for a bucket that serves a public website."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "ds314",
            "text": "3. Enable versioning before you need it. It protects against accidental overwrites and deletes, and lifecycle rules can expire old versions to control cost."
          },
          {
            "kind": "card",
            "id": "ds316",
            "children": [
              {
                "kind": "styled",
                "id": "ds315",
                "text": "$ aws s3 mb s3://northstar-build-artifacts --region us-west-2\n$ aws s3 cp ./atlas-0.1.1.tar.gz s3://northstar-build-artifacts/releases/\nupload: ./atlas-0.1.1.tar.gz to s3://northstar-build-artifacts/releases/atlas-0.1.1.tar.gz\n$ aws s3 ls s3://northstar-build-artifacts/releases/\n2026-09-12 09:41:07   18432011 atlas-0.1.1.tar.gz",
                "style": {
