          {
            "kind": "heading",
            "id": "dia10",
            "text": "IAM users, groups, and roles",
            "level": 1
          },
          {
            "kind": "styled",
            "id": "dia11",
            "text": "How identities and permissions work, and what to create for people versus workloads.",
            "style": {
              "size": 16,
              "color": "#545b64"
            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "dia12",
            "text": "1. An IAM user is a long-term identity with a password for the console or access keys for the CLI and API. Put users in groups and attach policies to the groups, not to the users. Require MFA for everyone."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "dia13",
            "text": "2. A role is an identity that is assumed, not signed into. EC2 instances, Lambda functions, and people federating from an identity provider assume roles and receive temporary credentials. Prefer roles over access keys for anything that runs on AWS."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "dia14",
            "text": "3. Policies are JSON documents that allow or deny actions on resources. Start from least privilege: grant the specific actions a task needs on the specific resources, and use IAM Access Analyzer to find unused permissions."
          },
          {
            "kind": "card",
            "id": "dia16",
            "children": [
              {
                "kind": "styled",
                "id": "dia15",
                "text": "{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n    \"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\", \"s3:PutObject\"],\n    \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::northstar-build-artifacts/releases/*\"\n  }]\n}",
                "style": {
